<article class="env-lemma" id="0CNA">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0CNA">Lemma <span data-tag="0CNA">29.47.4</span>.</a> Let $A \subset B$ be a ring extension. If there exists $b \in B$, $b \not\in A$ and an integer $n \geq 2$ with $b^ n \in A$ and $b^{n + 1} \in A$, then there exists a $b' \in B$, $b' \not\in A$ with $(b')^2 \in A$ and $(b')^3 \in A$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $b$ and $n$ be as in the lemma. Then all sufficiently large powers of $b$ are in $A$. Namely, $(b^ n)^ k(b^{n + 1})^ i = b^{(k + i)n + i}$ which implies any power $b^ m$ with $m \geq n^2$ is in $A$. Hence if $i \geq 1$ is the largest integer such that $b^ i \not\in A$, then $(b^ i)^2 \in A$ and $(b^ i)^3 \in A$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>